{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966785,"nonce":"oIw+07Ck9IHN3WYnpyA/GUgXyTI=","title":"Eager css_anim_store drain in remove_subtree_nodes restarted survivor animations on","message":"`remove_subtree_nodes` used to call `css_anim_store.animations.remove(\u0026stable_id)` and `transitions.remove(\u0026stable_id)` eagerly for every torn-down node BEFORE the post-rebuild `mint_stable_ids_walk` ran. That violated the stable-key contract that every other stable-keyed subsystem in the renderer follows.\n\n**Symptom**: opening a sub-menu inside an open `cn::context_menu` made the parent menu's opacity flicker (drop near zero, then fade back over `--duration-fast`). Same shape for `cn::dropdown_menu` / `cn::menubar` submenus.\n\n**Mechanism**:\n1. Hover the submenu-trigger row → `spawn_submenu()` → `OverlayStack::push` → stack dirty bit + redraw.\n2. Frame loop queues a `RebuildKind::Structural` rebuild against `OVERLAY_STACK_LAYER_ID`.\n3. The Structural branch tears down EVERY existing child of the overlay layer via `remove_subtree_nodes` (including the survivor root menu's wrapper) and drains its `css_anim_store` entry.\n4. New subtree builds; `mint_stable_ids_walk` re-derives stable ids. The survivor wrapper re-mints with the EXACT same StableNodeId (same parent stable, same sibling_index because Vec::push only appends, same `cn-context-menu-{handle_id}` widget_key captured into the content closure at `show()` time).\n5. `start_all_css_animations` runs; its `contains_key(\u0026stable_id)` gate sees the just-drained empty slot and falls through to `start_css_animation_for_class`, which unconditionally inserts a fresh `ActiveCssAnimation::new(animation)`. The constructor calls `animation.start()` → t=0.\n6. Phase 4 applies the frame-1 opacity (≈0) to the survivor → visible flicker.\n\nThe CSS `:has(:hover) { animation: none }` rules in `cn_styles.rs` were dead code for this case because (a) `start_css_animation_for_class` reads only the base class rule via `stylesheet.get_class(name)` and never consults state/complex-selector overrides; (b) state-style passes only write to `RenderProps` and never call into `css_anim_store`.\n\n**Fix**: Stop the eager drain. Mirror `sweep_stale_handlers`:\n```rust\npub(crate) fn sweep_stale_css_animations(\u0026mut self) {\n    let valid: HashSet\u003cStableNodeId\u003e = self.stable_to_layout.keys().copied().collect();\n    if let Ok(mut store) = self.css_anim_store.lock() {\n        store.animations.retain(|stable, _| valid.contains(stable));\n        store.transitions.retain(|stable, _| valid.contains(stable));\n    }\n}\n```\nCall it after `mint_stable_ids_walk` in every rebuild path (build_element, Structural branch of process_pending_subtree_rebuilds_routed, rebuild_children_in_place). Survivors keep their `ActiveCssAnimation` so the gate suppresses restart; truly-gone stable ids drop.\n\n**Why**: stable-keying means stable_id is the survival contract; eager-drain in `remove_subtree_nodes` ran BEFORE re-mint and conflated \"node removed\" with \"node about to be re-registered under the same stable id\". Handlers and motion already use post-mint sweeps; only CSS animations diverged.\n\n**How to apply**: Whenever you add a new stable-keyed subsystem to RenderTree, write a `sweep_stale_X` that retains by membership in `stable_to_layout` AFTER mint. Never drain by stable id inside `remove_subtree_nodes` — by the time it runs, the new tree doesn't exist yet, so you can't tell survivor from genuinely-gone.\n\nRelated: [[gotcha-first-interaction-skips-class-animation]] is a separate first-open race; this fix does not address it.","files":null}]}